{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["The Torah commanded, <i>until the matter comes before the Elohim</i> (Exodus 22:8), and <i>and he shall pay as the judges determine</i> (Exodus 21:22) — if so, there is a commandment that Israel shall have judges. And He explained here that they shall appoint judges in all their cities when HaShem gives them the land, for outside the Land they are not obligated to appoint courts for them. But when the wronged person cries out, those qualified to judge will stand for him and judge him according to their laws, or he will go up to the Land in its time and there they will judge him in the place of judgment. And He added here <i>officers</i> — these are ones who will enforce the matter of judgment. And accordingly, Israel outside the Land are not commanded to appoint judges in their towns, and so the Rav, Rabbi Moshe, wrote (Hilkhot Sanhedrin 1:2). But in tractate Makkot (Makkot 7a) they learned: <i>And these shall be to you as a statute of judgment throughout your generations in all your dwellings</i> — this teaches that the Sanhedrin functions in the Land and outside the Land. If so, why is it said <i>judges and officers shall you appoint for yourself in all your gates</i>? Rather: in the Land you establish them in every district and in every city; outside the Land you establish them in every district but not in every city. And it appears from this that they are obligated to appoint a Sanhedrin outside the Land, and not in every city as in the Land of Israel, but district by district. If so, this commandment applies at all times regarding monetary laws and matters judged outside the Land. But in this time, after the <i>semikhah</i> (ordination) has been abolished, since all judgments are nullified from the Torah — as it is written <i>before them</i> (Leviticus 19:15) and not before laymen, and we are laymen — and there are no judges outside the Land except by rabbinic enactment, as we made their agents (Gittin 88b), we are not obligated in the commandment of appointing judges from the Torah at all." "And the sense of <b>for your tribes</b> is that it refers back to <i>you shall appoint for yourself</i> — it teaches that courts are established for every tribe and in every city, in the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 16:18), and so in tractate Sanhedrin (Sanhedrin 16b). And I do not understand the meaning of this thing, for since we appoint courts in every city, behold there are many courts in every tribe. Perhaps it comes to say that if one city belonged to two tribes, such as Jerusalem, which has a portion for Judah and Benjamin, that they should establish in it two courts. And so they raised in the chapter <i>Helek</i> (Sanhedrin 111a–b) that one city may be divided between two tribes, and thus Jerusalem belonged to Judah and Benjamin. And it is possible to explain that the verse obligated appointing a court for the entire tribe, and it would judge all of them, and afterwards a court is appointed in every city that would judge the city. And even though all of them are equal in number — twenty-three for capital cases and three for monetary cases — but the greatest among them in wisdom are appointed over the entire tribe, and beneath them for every city, and the litigants cannot compel one another to be judged except before the court of their own city, not before the court of another city. Even if both litigants were in another city, one can say, let us go before the court of our city; but the tribal court can compel all the people of its tribe to be judged before it, and even if the parties being judged are in their own city, it can say to the great court of the tribe, let us go. And likewise if the city courts were in doubt, they come before the great court of the tribe and ask, just as the Great Sanhedrin is appointed over all the courts of all Israel, so too one court is appointed over every tribe. And if they needed to enact and decree a matter upon their tribe, they decree and enact, and it is for the tribe like the decree of the Great Sanhedrin upon all Israel. And this court is the one mentioned in tractate Horayot (Horayot 5a), where we learned: if a court of one of the tribes ruled and that tribe acted according to their words, that tribe is liable and the rest of the tribes are exempt, etc. And according to the plain sense, the reading of the verse — <i>judges and officers shall you appoint for yourself, for your tribes, in all your gates</i> — one would say that they shall give judges to their tribes and they shall judge in all their gates, and the judge of the tribe shall judge in all his gates."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:50:50.026468911Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}